From b2d8110f0b8acd872da5414f8812633bec2aef6d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gus Bourg <gus@bourg.net>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 08:15:05 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 093/145] media: meson: vdec: hevc: never recycle the on-screen
 FBC generation

Under real CMA exhaustion the board took a page-allocator BUG in an
unrelated process - a 2 GiB write, no video playing, 49 minutes after the
decoder had been driven hard:

  Oops  pc: is_free_buddy_page  Comm: dd
    expand -> __rmqueue_pcplist -> get_page_from_freelist
    -> __folio_alloc -> ext4_da_write_begin -> vfs_write
  CMA: => 6212 free of 229376 pages

Reproduced deliberately with debug_pagealloc=on slub_debug=FZP: a seek
storm to drain CMA followed by a heavy write faults in the buddy
allocator's own integrity check.  slub_debug stayed quiet, so it is not a
slab overflow; debug_pagealloc did not trap a store, so it is not the CPU
writing through a stale pointer.  A corrupted struct page found later by
expand() is what a page freed while still in use looks like.

fbc_chunk_get_pressure_locked() recycles park[0] as well as park[1].
park[0] is the generation the session that just stopped was displaying -
its chunks back CAPTURE buffers that can still be on screen, which is the
entire reason it is parked rather than freed.  Splicing it onto the free
list does two things: a new session's firmware is handed memory the
display is scanning out, and the chunks become reachable from the free
list, so the next codec_hevc_free_fbc_buffers() - which ends in
fbc_chunks_release_locked(&fbc_pool.free) - dma_free_coherent()s them
while they are still referenced.  Those pages return to CMA live, and the
corruption surfaces whenever the allocator next hands them out.

Recycle only park[1].  It has already been displaced by a newer
generation and is precisely what free_fbc_buffers() releases on the next
session stop, so reusing it under pressure matches that lifetime.  When
that is not enough, fail the allocation: -ENOMEM propagates out of
codec_hevc_setup_buffers() and fails the session cleanly, which is a far
better outcome than corrupting memory the driver does not own.

Signed-off-by: Gus Bourg <gus@bourg.net>
---
 .../media/meson/vdec/codec_hevc_common.c      | 27 ++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_hevc_common.c b/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_hevc_common.c
index 0fadc53c1fac..55e0704cc565 100644
--- a/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_hevc_common.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_hevc_common.c
@@ -150,12 +150,33 @@ static struct fbc_chunk *fbc_chunk_get_pressure_locked(struct device *dev)
 	if (c)
 		return c;
 
-	if (list_empty(&fbc_pool.park[0]) && list_empty(&fbc_pool.park[1]))
+	/*
+	 * Only the aged-out generation may be recycled.
+	 *
+	 * park[0] is what the session that just stopped was displaying, and
+	 * the CAPTURE buffers it backs can still be on screen - that is the
+	 * entire reason it is parked instead of freed.  Handing those chunks
+	 * to a new session lets its firmware DMA into memory the display is
+	 * scanning out and, worse, leaves them on the pool's free list, where
+	 * the next codec_hevc_free_fbc_buffers() dma_free_coherent()s them
+	 * while they are still referenced.  Those pages go back to CMA live,
+	 * and the damage surfaces later in whatever the allocator hands them
+	 * to next - a BUG in set_buddy_order()/is_free_buddy_page() reached
+	 * from an unrelated process's write path.
+	 *
+	 * park[1] has already been displaced by a newer generation, and is
+	 * exactly what free_fbc_buffers() releases on the next session stop,
+	 * so reusing it here matches that lifetime.
+	 *
+	 * If that is not enough, fail: a clean -ENOMEM is a far better
+	 * outcome than corrupting memory this driver does not own.
+	 */
+	if (list_empty(&fbc_pool.park[1]))
 		return NULL;
 
 	list_splice_tail_init(&fbc_pool.park[1], &fbc_pool.free);
-	list_splice_tail_init(&fbc_pool.park[0], &fbc_pool.free);
-	dev_warn_once(dev, "CMA pressure: recycled parked FBC chunks\n");
+	dev_warn_once(dev,
+		      "CMA pressure: recycled the aged-out FBC generation\n");
 
 	return fbc_chunk_get_locked(dev);
 }
